  <data name="InfoApi.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The local REST and SignalR bridge synchronizes solution and runtime state between MOBAflow, MOBAsmart and other integrations. It serves plain HTTP without sign-in to devices on the home network.</value></data>
  <data name="InfoApiTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>MOBApi</value></data>
  <data name="InfoApplications.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Applications</value></data>
  <data name="InfoCapabilities.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Key capabilities</value></data>
  <data name="InfoCapabilityAutomation.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>• Journeys, feedback sequences, timetable decisions and visual workflow automation</value></data>
  <data name="InfoCapabilityFeedback.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>• Direct Z21 control, feedback events, track power and live telemetry</value></data>
  <data name="InfoCapabilityFleet.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>• Locomotive functions F0-F31, fleet photos and decoder records</value></data>
  <data name="InfoCapabilityMobile.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>• Mobile synchronization without pairing and preview-stage remote displays</value></data>
  <data name="InfoCapabilityRecorder.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>• Event recording with filtered export and structurally isolated replay</value></data>
  <data name="InfoCapabilitySpeech.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>• Offline Piper TTS, Windows Speech announcements and reusable audio actions</value></data>
  <data name="InfoCapabilityTrackPlan.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>• Track-plan editing, topology validation, direct turnout and signal controls, and block occupancy observations</value></data>
  <data name="InfoDesktop.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Windows control center creates, operates and monitors layouts. It includes train control, rolling-stock records, journeys and timetables, workflows, track plans, direct turnout and signal controls, block occupancy, monitoring, and the Recorder with isolated replay.</value></data>
  <data name="InfoDesktopTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>MOBAflow Desktop</value></data>
  <data name="InfoDisplay.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The display stack provides RGB565 rendering, a correlated UDP protocol and ESP32-S3 firmware with protected local provisioning. Remote-display integration remains under active development.</value></data>
  <data name="InfoDisplayTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>MOBAdisplay</value></data>
  <data name="InfoDocs.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>User documentation</value></data>
  <data name="InfoDocs.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open the MOBAflow user documentation</value></data>
  <data name="InfoHardware.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>Hardware and liability notes</value></data>
  <data name="InfoHardware.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open the hardware and liability notes</value></data>
  <data name="InfoLicense.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>MIT License</value></data>
  <data name="InfoLicense.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open the MIT license</value></data>
  <data name="InfoLinks.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Project resources</value></data>
  <data name="InfoMobile.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Android companion provides lap counting, locomotive control, the synchronized signal box, fleet information and photo capture close to the layout. It connects to MOBApi on the home network without pairing or sign-in.</value></data>
  <data name="InfoMobileTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>MOBAsmart</value></data>
  <data name="InfoNetwork.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>MOBAflow, MOBAsmart, MOBApi and MOBAdisplay are intended for a trusted private LAN. Direct exposure of MOBApi or display endpoints to the public internet is unsupported.</value></data>
  <data name="InfoNews.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>MOBAnews</value></data>
  <data name="InfoNews.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open MOBAnews</value></data>
  <data name="InfoPreview.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Preview features are available for testing and may still change. Hardware acceptance remains the responsibility of the layout operator.</value></data>
  <data name="InfoProduct.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>MOBAflow</value></data>
  <data name="InfoPurpose.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>MOBAflow turns feedback from a Roco Z21 layout into useful actions. It controls trains, advances journeys, coordinates timetable operations, executes reusable workflows, plays local announcements and updates signals while project data and layout communication remain on the local network.</value></data>
  <data name="InfoPurposeTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What MOBAflow does</value></data>
  <data name="InfoRepository.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>GitHub repository</value></data>
  <data name="InfoRepository.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open the MOBAflow GitHub repository</value></data>
  <data name="InfoRoadmap.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>Roadmap</value></data>
  <data name="InfoRoadmap.[using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Automation]AutomationProperties.Name" xml:space="preserve"><value>Open the MOBAflow roadmap</value></data>
  <data name="InfoSafety.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>MOBAflow can send commands to real model railroad hardware. It does not reserve routes, lock turnouts or prevent collisions. The operator is responsible for safe operation, including signal settings and stopping behavior.</value></data>
  <data name="InfoSafetyTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Safety and network scope</value></data>
  <data name="InfoTagline.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Event-driven automation for model railroads</value></data>
  <data name="InfoTrademarks.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>MOBAflow is an independent open-source project. Product names and trademarks belong to their respective owners.</value></data>
